{"id": "q0160", "pool": "frozen", "arm": "local", "tier": "never", "lang": "en", "run_id": "ttfa1", "attempt": 1, "seed": 4242962681, "protocol": "ttfa-v3", "config_sha256": "923cbbdfe3213927dfa36b1f8e0eb48a30d641d9b9bf7cf25d16765340d88836", "gate_art_sha256": "0e6494c2eeac9bcd86c10b5def3cbd32e98bb0765fa2fd8afc8c1b47915ea372", "threshold": null, "gpu": "NVIDIA H100 80GB HBM3", "host": "modal", "shard": 5, "query": " Consider the transition between two forms of solid tin, $\\mathrm{Sn}(s$, gray $) \\rightarrow \\mathrm{Sn}(s$, white $)$. The two phases are in equilibrium at 1 bar and $18^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}$. The densities for gray and white tin are 5750 and $7280 \\mathrm{~kg} \\mathrm{~m}^{-3}$, respectively, and the molar entropies for gray and white tin are 44.14 and $51.18 \\mathrm{~J} \\mathrm{~K}^{-1} \\mathrm{~mol}^{-1}$, respectively. Calculate the temperature at which the two phases are in equilibrium at 350. bar.\n\nA. 5.2 $^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}$\nB. -7.8 $^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}$\nC. -3.5 $^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}$\nD. 0.0 $^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}$\nE. 21.4 $^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}$\nF. -10.0 $^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}$\nG. 10.0 $^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}$\nH. -15.2 $^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}$\nI. -25.0 $^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}$\nJ. 13.7 $^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}$\n\nAnswer with the correct option letter and its content.", "input_path": "/data/audio_pool/q0160.wav", "input_sha256": "be940326112268860e32271ab7300efe2524c9efaeb7d08c0a1020fb54e3ba81", "input_samples": 949600, "input_s": 59.35, "n_real_chunks": 60, "score": 0.787, "act": 0.9974, "is_info": true, "fired": false, "mode": "local", "answer": "Okay, so this is a phase equilibrium problem involving the transition between gray and white tin. We're given data at 1 bar and 18 ° C, but we need to find the temperature where they' re in equilibrium at 3 50 bar. First, I should recall that for two phases in equilibrium, the chemical potential s must be equal. The Gibbs free energy change for the transition can be related to entropy and volume changes. Since pressure is changing, I might need to use the Cl apeyron equation or something similar. Let me think about the molar volumes first. Density is mass over volume, so molar volume would be mo lar mass divided by density. Tin has a molar mass around 118 g/mol, which I'll use as an approximation. For gray tin, V_g ray = M / rho_ gray = 1 18 / 5 7 50 cm^ 3/ g * 10 00 to convert to m^3? Actually, since we're dealing with ratios, maybe I can keep it in cm^3 for now. Similarly, V_ white = 1 18 / 7 2", "relay_text": "", "expert_answer": "", "transcript": "", "asr_s": null, "expert_s": null, "expert_input_samples": null, "expert_input_s": null, "expert_input_sha256": null, "expert_timed_out": false, "wait_s": null, "relay_synth_ms": null, "onset_chunk": 59, "eot_seen": false, "n_speak_chunks": 60, "feed_lag_max_ms": 0, "feed_lag_sum_ms": 0, "audio_files": {"local": {"path": "/data/ttfa_real/ttfa1/frozen/audio/q0160_local_local.wav", "sha256": "eb4eda4767a1d7ebca57790aed1b5f6aa0f53a509360244e3de1f73c84380d16", "sr": 24000, "seconds": 60.0}}, "chunks": [[0, 1, 1.0, 1.056, 1.101, 1, 0, 0], [1, 1, 2.0, 2.06, 2.107, 1, 0, 0], [2, 1, 3.0, 3.059, 3.106, 1, 0, 0], [3, 1, 4.0, 4.06, 4.106, 1, 0, 0], [4, 1, 5.0, 5.06, 5.107, 1, 0, 0], [5, 1, 6.0, 6.059, 6.106, 1, 0, 0], [6, 1, 7.0, 7.06, 7.108, 1, 0, 0], [7, 1, 8.0, 8.058, 8.103, 1, 0, 0], [8, 1, 9.0, 9.06, 9.109, 1, 0, 0], [9, 1, 10.0, 10.062, 10.107, 1, 0, 0], [10, 1, 11.0, 11.06, 11.108, 1, 0, 0], [11, 1, 12.0, 12.06, 12.107, 1, 0, 0], [12, 1, 13.0, 13.062, 13.11, 1, 0, 0], [13, 1, 14.0, 14.061, 14.109, 1, 0, 0], [14, 1, 15.0, 15.06, 15.109, 1, 0, 0], [15, 1, 16.0, 16.062, 16.108, 1, 0, 0], [16, 1, 17.0, 17.062, 17.113, 1, 0, 0], [17, 1, 18.0, 18.062, 18.109, 1, 0, 0], [18, 1, 19.0, 19.064, 19.113, 1, 0, 0], [19, 1, 20.0, 20.062, 20.111, 1, 0, 0], [20, 1, 21.0, 21.063, 21.11, 1, 0, 0], [21, 1, 22.0, 22.063, 22.112, 1, 0, 0], [22, 1, 23.0, 23.062, 23.11, 1, 0, 0], [23, 1, 24.0, 24.063, 24.11, 1, 0, 0], [24